.SH "NAME"
\fBlgdb\fP - line mode parallel debugger
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.PP
.nf
\fB\%lgdb\fP
[\fB-b\fP \fIfile\fP | \fB--batch\fP=\fIfile\fP]
[\fB-h\fP | \fB--help\fP]
[\fB-o\fP \fIfile\fP | \fB--output\fP=\fIfile\fP]
[\fB-s\fP | \fB--silent\fP]
[\fB-v\fP | \fB--version\fP]
.fi
.SH "IMPLEMENTATION"
.PP
Cray\ Linux\ Environment (CLE)
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
.PP
\fB\%lgdb\fP is a GDB-based parallel debugger used to debug applications compiled with CCE, PGI, GNU and Intel Fortran, C and C++ compilers\&. It allows programmers to either launch an application or attach to an already-running application\&.
.PP
\fB\%lgdb\fP also includes \fIcomparative debugger\fR technology that enables programmers to compare data structures between two executing applications\&. Cray, however, recommends accessing the comparative debugger technology through the new Cray Comparative Debugger (CCDB) with graphical user interface (GUI) that enhances the parallel debugging capabilities of \fB\%lgdb\fP\&. For further information on CCDB, see \fB\%ccdb\fR(1)\&.
.SS "Options"
.PP
The \fB\%lgdb\fP debugger accepts the following options:
.TP 10
\fB-b\fP \fIfile\fP
.PD 0
.TP 10
\fB--batch\fP=\fIfile\fP
.PD
Executes debugger commands from \fIfile\fP, then exits\&.
.TP 10
\fB-h\fP
.PD 0
.TP 10
\fB--help\fP
.PD
Displays command line help\&.
.TP 10
\fB-o\fP \fIfile\fP
.PD 0
.TP 10
\fB--output\fP=\fIfile\fP
.PD
Redirects \fBstdout\fP/\fBstderr\fP to \fIfile\fP\&. Must be used with \fB--batch\fP\&.
.TP 10
\fB-s\fP
.PD 0
.TP 10
\fB--silent\fP
.PD
Suppresses version banner on startup\&.
.TP 10
\fB-v\fP
.PD 0
.TP 10
\fB--version\fP
.PD
Displays version\&.
.SS "\fB\%lgdb\fP Usage"
.PP
\fBProcedure 1\&. To begin using \fB\%lgdb\fP\fR
.PP
\fIStep 1\fR.  Load the \fBcray-lgdb\fP module if it is not already loaded\&.
.nf
.sp
.po +2
% \fBmodule load cray-lgdb\fP
.po -2
.fi
.sp
.PP
\fIStep 2\fR.  Compile the application using either the \fB-g\fP or \fB-G\fP\fIn\fP option of the relevant compiler to include additional debugger information required by \fB\%lgdb\fP\&. For example, if compiling the Fortran application \fB\%myprog42\&.f90\fP, enter this command:
.nf
.sp
.po +2
% \fBftn -g -o myapp_42 myprog42\&.f90\fP
.po -2
.fi
.sp
.IP "" 10
If using the comparative debugging features with CCE, it is recommended to add \fB-e0 -ez\fP to the \fB\%ftn\fP compile line or \fB-h zero\fP to the \fB\%cc\fP compile line to force memory initialization to zero\&. This helps to avoid false positive differences due to comparing uninitialized memory\&.
.PP
\fIStep 3\fR.  For systems that normally operate in batch mode only, Cray \fB\%lgdb\fP has three methods for interacting with the supported WLM systems (ALPS, hybrid SLURM, and native SLURM):
.IP "\(bu" 3
Interactive batch: Prior to launching \fB\%lgdb\fP, request the compute node resources required for the debugging session\&. For example, if PBS Pro is the site\&'s batch system, enter this command:
.nf
.sp
% \fB\%qsub -IV -l mppwidth=\fIw\fP\fR
.fi
.sp
.IP "" 3
Where \fIw\fP is the number of ranks required\&. Then after initiating \fB\%lgdb\fP, subsequent use of \fB\%lgdb\fP commands will employ these resources\&.
.IP "\(bu" 3
The \fB\%launch\fP command\&'s \fB--qsub\fP or \fB--sbatch\fP options will cause a batch job to be submitted\&. See \fIWorkload Manager Support\fR for more information about modifying existing job scripts to work with \fB\%lgdb\fP\&.
.IP "\(bu" 3
The \fB\%session\fP command creates a new WLM session that will be associated with all future \fB\%launch\fP commands\&. See \fIWorkload Manager Support\fR for more information about modifying existing job scripts to work with \fB\%lgdb\fP\&.
.PP
.RS
\fBNote:\fR  If using the \fB\%launch\fP command multiple times to launch applications with either the interactive batch method or the \fB\%session\fP command method under the ALPS reservation system, keep the following in mind when determining the number of PEs to request - ALPS will allocate whole increments of compute nodes to the applications regardless of the number of PEs (\fB\%aprun -n\fP argument) requested\&. For example, if running on a system with 32 PEs per compute node and using process handles \fB$a{8}\fP and \fB$b{8}\fP, the user should request 64 PEs, or 8 PEs per node, using WLM arguments to ensure enough PEs are allocated to satisfy both \fB\%launch\fP commands\&.
.RE
.PP
\fIStep 4\fR.  The application\&'s WLM-specific application identifier is needed to attach to an application that was previously launched outside of \fB\%lgdb\fP\&. For ALPS, this is the application process ID (\fBapid\fP); for native SLURM, this is a combination of the job ID (\fBjob_id\fP) and the job step ID (\fBstep_id\fP)\&.
.IP "" 10
For ALPS, use the \fB\%apstat\fP command to determine the application\&'s \fBapid\fP\&. For example, if the application \fBmyapp_42\fP was launched using \fB\%aprun\fP, the following \fB\%apstat\fP output shows the application\&'s \fBapid\fP is \fB651486\fP\&.
.nf
.sp
.po +2
% \fBapstat\fP
Compute node summary
   arch config     up    use   held  avail   down
     XT   1072   1066     24     49    993      6

No pending applications are present

Total placed applications: 3
Placed  Apid ResId     User   PEs Nodes    Age   State Command
      \fI651486\fP    60  user123    64     8   0h27m  run   \fImyapp_42\fP
      651488    61  user123    64     8   0h27m  run   app_LIB
      651490    62  user124     8     8   0h06m  run   IOR
.po -2
.fi
.sp
For native SLURM, use the \fB\%qstat\fP and \fBsstat\fP commands to obtain the \fBjob_id\fP and \fBstep_id\fP, respectively\&. The SLURM application identifier should be provided as \fIjob_id\fP\&.\fIstep_id\fP\&. For example, if the application \fBa\&.out\fP was launched using \fB\%qsub\fP, the following \fB\%qstat\fP output shows the application\&'s \fBjob_id\fP is \fB453282\fP, and the \fB\%sstat\fP output shows the application\&'s \fBstep_id\fP is \fB0\fP\&.
.nf
.sp
.po +2
% \fBqstat\fP
Job id              Name             Username        Time Use S Queue
------------------- ---------------- --------------- -------- - ---------------
453224              sst              user123         00:01:59 R share
453282              a\&.out            user124         00:00:00 R share
453283              Chpl-lulesh-den  user125         00:00:00 R share

% \fBsstat 453282\fP
JobID     MaxVMSize  MaxVMSizeNode  MaxVMSizeTask  AveVMSize  MaxRSS \&.\&.\&.
453282\&.0  226136K    nid00013       30             204001K    9936K  \&.\&.\&.      
.po -2
.fi
.sp
.PP
\fIStep 5\fR.  Use the \fB\%lgdb\fP command to launch the parallel debugger\&.
.nf
.sp
.po +2
% \fBlgdb\fP
.po -2
.fi
.sp
.IP "" 10
The system responds as follows:
.nf
.sp
.po +2
lgdb 2\&.4 - Cray Line Mode Parallel Debugger 
With Cray Comparative Debugging Technology\&.
Copyright 2007-2014 Cray Inc\&. All Rights Reserved\&.
Copyright 1996-2013 University of Queensland\&. All rights Reserved\&.

Type "help" for a list of commands\&.
Type "help <cmd>" for detailed help about a command\&.
dbg all>
.po -2
.fi
.sp
.PP
\fIStep 6\fR.  Once \fB\%lgdb\fP is launched and the \fBdbg all>\fP prompt is displayed, the program uses a command-line interface similar to that used by \fB\%gdb\fP\&. Enter \fBhelp\fP for more information about supported commands\&.
.nf
.sp
.po +2
dbg all> \fBhelp\fP
assign          Change the value of an application or debugger variable\&.
attach          Attach to an application\&.
backtrace       Print backtrace of all stack frames\&.
break           Set breakpoint at specified line or function\&.
build           Build an assertion script\&.
compare         Compare the contents of two variables\&.
continue        Continue execution of application\&.
decomposition   Define a decomposition scheme\&.
defset          Create a set of processes\&.
delete          Delete a breakpoint\&.
disable         Disable a breakpoint\&.
down            Move down one or more stack frames\&.
enable          Enable a breakpoint\&.
finish          Execute application until each rank\&'s current function returns\&.
focus           Set the current process set\&.
frame           Print the currently selected stack frame\&.
gdbmode         Enter direct dgb mode (experimental)\&.
halt            Halt execution of attached/launched application\&.
help            Display help information about commands\&.
info            Display information about the application being debugged\&.
kill            Kill attached/launched application\&.
launch          Launch an application\&.
list            List source for specified function or line\&.
maint           Commands for use by debugger maintainers\&.
next            Step application, proceeding through subroutine calls\&.
print           Print the value of an expression\&.
quit            Exit the debugger\&.
release         Release attached/launched application from debugger control\&.
session         Create a new WLM session\&.
set             Set information about the debugger environment\&.
show            Show information about the debugger environment\&.
source          Read debugger commands from a file\&.
start           Start executing an assertion script\&.
step            Step application until it reaches a different source line\&.
stop            Stop the currently executing assertion script\&.
tbreak          Set a temporary breakpoint at a specified line or function\&.
unset           Unset information in the debugger environment\&.
up              Move up one or more stack frames\&.
usage           Display usage information about script mode commands\&.
viewset         Display information about process sets\&.
watch           Set watchpoint on specified expression\&.
whatis          Print the data type of an expression\&.
.po -2
.fi
.sp
.IP "" 10
To view detailed information about a specific command, enter \fB\%help \fIcommand\fP\fR\&. For example:
.nf
.sp
.po +2
dbg all> \fBhelp break\fP

Summary: Set breakpoint command\&.
Usage: break [location]
Example command: break main\&.c:20

Arguments: location(line number, function name, or address)

Set a breakpoint in every application rank in the current process set\&.
The location of the breakpoint can be defined as a line number, a function
name, or "*" and an address\&. If an address is specified, it must begin
with an asterix (*)\&. For example, break *0x0000000000400693 would break
at address 0x400693\&.
.po -2
.fi
.sp
.PP
\fIStep 7\fR.  When finished using \fB\%lgdb\fP, enter \fBquit\fP to exit the debugger\&. Upon exit, launched applications are killed (execution terminated) and attached applications are released from the debugger\&'s control but are allowed to continue execution\&. Applications can be killed or released from within \fB\%lgdb\fP, prior to exiting, with the \fB\%kill\fP or \fB\%release\fP commands, respectively\&.
.SS " Process Sets and Variables"
.PP
When an application is debugged using \fB\%lgdb\fP, the application ranks are arranged into a group called a \fIprocess set\fR\&. The default process set is named \fIall\fR and contains the ranks of every application that is currently being debugged\&. Debugger variables are used to reference process sets and are defined by the \fB\%attach\fP and \fB\%launch\fP commands\&. Debugger variables are denoted by a dollar sign (\fB$\fP) followed by the variable name, such as \fB$a\fP, to represent a scalar variable, or \fB$b{1024}\fP, with required squiggly braces, to represent an array variable\&. \fB$a\fP and \fB$b\fP are referred to as application \fIhandles\fR\&.
.PP
A user can refer to specific application ranks and to ranks from multiple invoked applications by using the \fB\%defset\fP command to define a new process set containing the desired ranks and then \fB\%focus\fP on that set\&. The expression defining the process set must be a comma-separated list of valid application handles and valid ranks\&. In the following examples, assume that \fB$a{64}\fP and \fB$b{64}\fP represent two launched applications\&.
.PP
\fBExample 1: Reference ranks from a single application\fR
.PP
To reference ranks 0 through 31 and 63 from \fB$a\fP, define a new process set \fB$c\fP\&. Note that "\fB\&.\&.\fP" denotes a range of ranks\&.
.nf
.sp
.po +2
dbg all> \fBdefset $c $a{0\&.\&.31,63}\fP
dbg all> \fBfocus $c\fP
dbg c>
.po -2
.fi
.sp
.PP
\fBExample 2: Reference ranks from an existing process set\fR
.PP
To reference ranks 0 through 2 and 63 based on process set \fB$c\fP defined in \fIExample 1: Reference ranks from a single application\fR, define a new process set \fB$d\fP\&. Note that "\fB\&.\&.\fP" denotes a range of ranks\&.
.nf
.sp
.po +2
dbg all> \fBdefset $d $c{0\&.\&.2,32}\fP
dbg all> \fBfocus $d\fP
dbg d> 
.po -2
.fi
.sp
The \fB\%viewset\fP command can be used to view which application ranks are in a defined process set\&. After issuing the commands from \fIExample 1: Reference ranks from a single application\fR and \fIExample 2: Reference ranks from an existing process set\fR, \fB\%viewset\fP shows:
.nf
.sp
.po +2
dbg d> \fBviewset\fP
Name     Procs
all     a{0\&.\&.63),b{0\&.\&.63}
a       a{0\&.\&.63}
b       b{0\&.\&.63}
c       a{0\&.\&.31,63}
d       a{0\&.\&.2,63}
.po -2
.fi
.sp
.PP
\fBExample 3: Reference ranks from multiple applications\fR
.PP
To reference ranks 0 through 31 and 63 from \fB$a\fP and ranks 31 and 63 from \fB$b\fP, define a new process set \fB$e\fP\&. Note that "\fB\&.\&.\fP" denotes a range of ranks\&.
.nf
.sp
.po +2
dbg all> \fBdefset $e $a{0\&.\&.31,63},$b{31,63}\fP
dbg all> \fBfocus $e\fP
dbg e>
.po -2
.fi
.sp
.PP
\fBExample 4: Focus on a temporary process set\fR
.PP
By using the \fB\%focus\fP command, a user can focus explicitly on a subset of ranks without defining a new process set\&. \fB\%lgdb\fP creates a temporary process set that can subsequently be used in process targeted commands\&. The temporary process set is removed when the focus is changed\&. Unlike defined process sets, a temporary process set can only contain ranks from one application\&.
.nf
.sp
.po +2
dbg all> \fBfocus $b{0,2,5\&.\&.8,31}\fP
dbg b_temp> \fBbt\fP
b{0,2,5\&.\&.8,31}: #0 0x00000000004010ff in main at /tmp/usr/test/c_test\&.c:132
dbg b_temp> \fBfocus $all\fP
dbg all>
.po -2
.fi
.sp
.PP
Finally, a user can refer to every rank of an application by focusing on the process handle that was specified when the application was first attached or launched\&. When a user focuses on a process set, it becomes the \fIcurrent set\fR\&.
.SS "Application Variables"
.PP
Application variables are referenced as follows:
.nf
.sp
.po +2
[$\fIproc_set\fP::]\fIvar\fP
.po -2
.fi
.sp
.PP
Where $\fIproc_set\fP is an optional process set identifier, and \fIvar\fP is the desired application variable\&. If no process set is declared, the executing command will act on all ranks in the current set\&. To reference variable \fBmax\fP in all ranks of the process set \fB$b\fP, use:
.nf
.sp
.po +2
$b::max
.po -2
.fi
.sp
.PP
To reference a rank-specific element, use:
.nf
.sp
.po +2
$b{4}::max
.po -2
.fi
.sp
.PP
To reference multiple rank-specific elements, use:
.nf
.sp
.po +2
$b{0\&.\&.3,12}::max
.po -2
.fi
.sp
Note that "\fB\&.\&.\fP" denotes a range of ranks\&.
.PP
To reference application variable arrays, use the application\&'s coding language syntax\&. For example, to reference elements of the array \fBxval\fP in rank 2 of process set $b, use:
.nf
.sp
.po +2
In Fortran - $b{2}::xval(3,2)
In c - $b{2}::xval[2][3]
.po -2
.fi
.sp
.SS "Launching an Application"
.PP
The \fB\%launch\fP command transparently invokes the WLM job launch command (i\&.e\&., \fB\%aprun\fP or \fB\%srun\fP) to launch an application\&. Following the launch, the application is held immediately before execution is passed to its \fBMPI_init()\fP, \fBshmem_init()\fP, or \fBmain()\fP routine and is ready for further \fB\%lgdb\fP commands\&. Integration with \fB\%qsub\fP and \fB\%sbatch\fP is also supported as described in \fIWorkload Manager Support\fR\&. Launching on systems whose default workload manager is not SLURM or ALPS, or launching an application using a non default launcher requires passwordless (public key) ssh to the compute nodes of active jobs\&. Contact a system administrator or the system usage guide for details on whether this is supported and how to set this up\&.
.PP
The simplest form of the \fB\%launch\fP command is:
.nf
.sp
.po +2
\fB\%launch\fP $\fIproc_set\fP \fIapplication\fP
.po -2
.fi
.sp
.PP
$\fIproc_set\fP is a single debugger variable array that specifies the number of application ranks to be launched\&. For example, the following will launch the application \fBtstapp\fP via the WLM job launcher with 1024 ranks (\fB-n1024\fP) assigned to the process set \fB$b\fP:
.nf
.sp
.po +2
dbg all> \fBlaunch $b{1024} tstapp\fP
.po -2
.fi
.sp
.PP
The \fB\%launch\fP command also accepts the following optional arguments\&. Option values must be enclosed within quotation marks, such as "args\&."
.TP 10
\fB--launcher=\fP "\fIlauncher_name\fP" | \fB-l\fP ``\fIlauncher_name\fP''
Launches the job with the launcher specified by \fIlauncher_name\fP\&.
.TP 10
\fB--launcher-args=\fP "\fIlauncher_args\fP" | \fB-g\fP ``\fIlauncher_args\fP''
Passes \fIlauncher_args\fP to the WLM job launch command\&.
.TP 10
\fB--launcher-input=\fP "\fIinput_file\fP" | \fB-i\fP ``\fIinput_file\fP''
Redirects the \fBstdin\fP of the WLM job launch command to be \fIinput_file\fP\&. This is useful for applications requiring input from \fBstdin\fP\&.
.TP 10
\fB--args=\fP "\fIapp_args\fP" | \fB-a\fP "\fIapp_args\fP"
Passes \fIapp_args\fP to the application executable\&.
.TP 10
\fB--env=\fP''\fIname\fP=\fIvalue\fP'',\fB--env=\fP''\fIname\fP=\fIvalue\fP'',\&.\&.\&.
Sets the environment variable (defined by \fIname\fP) to \fIvalue\fP, for this job session instance\&. Note that \fB--env=\fP can be used more than once to set multiple environment variables\&.
.TP 10
\fB--gpu\fP
Enables the use of an OpenACC supported version of \fB\%gdb\fP for debugging on a Nvidia CUDA 5\&.5 or above GPU\&. Sets several environment variables for debugging\&. 
.IP "" 10
\fBNote:\fP  OpenACC debugging is only supported through the \fB\%launch\fP command\&.
.TP 10
\fB--qsub=\fP''\fIbatch_template\fP''
Submits \fIbatch_template\fP as a PBS job from which an application will launch for debugging\&.
.TP 10
\fB--sbatch=\fP''\fIbatch_template\fP''
Submits \fIbatch_template\fP as a SLURM job from which an application will launch for debugging\&.
.TP 10
\fB--workdir=\fP "\fIwork_path\fP" | \fB-d\fP ``\fIwork_path\fP''
Changes the current working directory, relative to its present setting where \fB\%lgdb\fP was invoked, to \fIwork_path\fP\&. This is useful for applications that write files to the current working directory\&. By default, if the \fB--workdir=\fP option is specified without a path, the current working directory will be changed to the location of the application\&'s executable file\&.
.SS "Caveat for Launching a Serial Application"
.PP
Parallel applications contain a communication mechanism that causes a startup barrier code to be pulled in during compilation\&. \fB\%lgdb\fP uses the barrier code to hold the application at its entry point in preparation to begin the debugging process\&. Typically, serial applications do not have a barrier code and, therefore, must be modified in order to be debugged using \fB\%lgdb\fP\&.
.PP
Users can add either an \fBMPI_Init()\fP call or a fake startup barrier at the beginning of their code\&. For example, using C code:
.nf
.sp
.po +2
int dummyBarrier = 1;
while (dummyBarrier);
.po -2
.fi
.sp
After attaching to the application in \fB\%lgdb\fP, issue the following command and proceed to debug as usual:
.nf
.sp
.po +2
dbg all> \fBassign dummyBarrier 0\fP
.po -2
.fi
.sp
If the fake startup barrier method is utilized, the user must use the \fB\%attach\fP command; the \fB\%launch\fP is unavailable and will hang indefinitely\&.
.SS "Attaching to a Previously Launched Application"
.PP
Use the \fB\%attach\fP command to attach to an executing application that was launched outside of \fB\%lgdb\fP\&.
.nf
.sp
.po +2
dgb all> \fB\%attach $\fIproc_set\fP \fIapp_ident\fP\fR
.po -2
.fi
.sp
.PP
Where $\fIproc_set\fP is the debugger variable to be associated with the application, and \fIapp_ident\fP is the WLM-specific application identifier of the running application\&. $\fIproc_set\fP \fImust not\fP include array syntax; the size of the $\fIproc_set\fP array is automatically set to the number of ranks in the job\&. For ALPS, \fIapp_ident\fP is the application process ID (\fBapid\fP); for native SLURM, \fIapp_ident\fP is a combination of the job ID (\fBjob_id\fP) and the job step ID (\fBstep_id\fP) and must be provided as \fIjob_id\fP\&.\fIstep_id\fP\&.
.PP
The following command instructs \fB\%lgdb\fP to associate the debugger variable \fB$tst42\fP with the application that has \fBapid\fP 651486\&.
.nf
.sp
.po +2
dbg all> \fBattach $tst42 651486\fP
.po -2
.fi
.sp
.SS "Workload Manager Support"
.PP
Two methods exist for submitting batch jobs directly to a WLM:
.IP "\(bu" 3
The \fB\%launch\fP command\&'s \fB\%--qsub\fP or \fB\%--sbatch\fP options can be used to submit a PBS or SLURM job, respectively, from within which an application will be launched for debugging\&. 
.IP "\(bu" 3
The \fB\%session\fP command will create a new WLM session to which all future \fB\%launch\fP or \fB\%attach\fP commands will be associated\&. This allows multiple \fB\%launch\fP commands to be used in the same batch session\&. \fB\%session\fP accepts the same \fB\%--qsub\fP or \fB\%--sbatch\fP options as \fB\%launch\fP\&. 
.IP "" 3
For either method, the existing application job script is first modified by placing the comments \fB#cray_debug_start\fP and \fB#cray_debug_end\fP around the job launch command (i\&.e\&., \fB\%aprun\fP or \fB\%srun\fP) that is to be debugged\&. Everything between the comment lines will be ignored; the original WLM job launch command must be recreated through \fB\%lgdb\fP\&'s \fB\%launch\fP command arguments\&. For example, the job script \fB\%sample\&.pbs\fP is modified with the \fB\%lgdb\fP comments, for ALPS:
.nf
.sp
#cray_debug_start
aprun -n128 -N32 a\&.out
#cray_debug_end
.fi
.sp
For SLURM:
.nf
.sp
#cray_debug_start
srun -n128 -nstacks-per-node=32 a\&.out
#cray_debug_end
.fi
.sp
.IP "" 3
The following \fB\%launch\fP command will then submit \fB\%sample\&.pbs\fP as a PBS job from within which the application \fB\%a\&.out\fP will be launched:
.nf
.sp
dgb all> \fBlaunch $a{128} --launcher-args="-N32" --qsub=sample\&.pbs a\&.out\fP
.fi
.sp
.IP "" 3
Using the \fB\%session\fP command, this would be accomplished as follows:
.nf
.sp
dgb all> \fBsession --qsub=sample\&.pbs\fP
dgb all> \fBlaunch $a{128} --launcher-args="-N32" a\&.out\fP
.fi
.sp
.IP "" 10
\fBNote:\fP  If either the \fB\%launch\fP or \fB\%session\fP command is interrupted before completion, the job reservation must be manually deleted from the queueing system\&.
.SH "ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES"
.TP 10
\fBCRAY_APRUN_PATH\fP
Defines the location of the Cray-provided \fB\%aprun\fP binary\&. Used if the \fB\%aprun\fP binary has been renamed or moved from its default location\&.
.IP "" 10
Default: not defined
.TP 10
\fBCRAY_LGDB_HIST_DIR\fP
Defines the directory into which the \fB\%\&.lgdb_history\fP file is located\&.
.IP "" 10
Default: not set
.TP 10
\fBCRAY_LGDB_PMI_INIT_SLEEP\fP
Sets extra time (in seconds) to sleep after finding the \fB\%pmi_attribs\fP file during early attach\&. This should only be set if \fB\%lgdb\fP is attaching to the application during the \fBpmi_init\fP function and causing the debugger to hang\&.
.IP "" 10
Default: not set
.TP 10
\fBCRAY_LGDB_STARTUP_TIMEOUT\fP
Sets the MRNet startup timeout in seconds\&.
.IP "" 10
Default: not set
.SH "EXAMPLES"
.PP
A demo directory with sample codes is included in the \fB\%lgdb\fP release\&. The following example uses the executable created by entering the command: \fBmake hello_mpi_c\fP\&.
.PP
Launch one instance of \fB\%hello_mpi_c\fP with 8 ranks\&.
.nf
.sp
.po +2
dbg all> \fBlaunch $tst{8} hello_mpi_c\fP
Starting alps application, please wait\&.\&.\&.
Creating MRNet communication network\&.\&.\&.
Waiting for debug servers to attach to MRNet communications network\&.\&.\&.
Timeout in 60 seconds\&. Please wait for the attach to complete\&.
Number of dbgsrvs connected: [1];  Timeout Counter: [0]
Number of dbgsrvs connected: [1];  Timeout Counter: [1]
Number of dbgsrvs connected: [8];  Timeout Counter: [0]
Finalizing setup\&.\&.\&.
Launch complete\&.
tst{0\&.\&.7}: Initial breakpoint, main at /lus/nid/user/tests/hello_mpi\&.c:16
dbg all>
.po -2
.fi
.sp
.PP
Set a breakpoint at line 21 of source file \fB\%hello_mpi\&.c\fP and continue execution\&.
.nf
.sp
.po +2
dbg all> \fBbreak hello_mpi\&.c:21\fP
tst{0\&.\&.7}: Breakpoint 1: file hello_mpi\&.c, line 21\&.
dbg all> \fBcontinue\fP
tst{0\&.\&.7}: Breakpoint 1, main at hello_mpi\&.c
dbg all>
.po -2
.fi
.sp
.PP
Print the value of myRank for each rank in the current process set\&.
.nf
.sp
.po +2
dbg all> \fBprint myRank\fP
tst{0}: 0
tst{1}: 1
tst{2}: 2
tst{3}: 3
tst{4}: 4
tst{5}: 5
tst{6}: 6
tst{7}: 7
.po -2
.fi
.sp
